// DSU with rollback
struct RollbackDSU {
    vector<int> p, sz;
    vector<pair<int,int>> st; // (b, old_sz_a), b=-1 means no-op
    int comps;

    RollbackDSU(int n = 0) { init(n); }

    void init(int n) {
        p.resize(n);
        sz.assign(n, 1);
        iota(p.begin(), p.end(), 0);
        st.clear();
        comps = n;
    }

    int find(int x) {
        while (p[x] != x) x = p[x];
        return x;
    }

    void unite(int a, int b) {
        a = find(a), b = find(b);
        if (a == b) {
            st.push_back({-1, -1});
            return;
        }
        if (sz[a] < sz[b]) swap(a, b);
        st.push_back({b, sz[a]});
        p[b] = a;
        sz[a] += sz[b];
        comps--;
    }

    int snapshot() const {
        return (int)st.size();
    }

    void rollback(int snap) {
        while ((int)st.size() > snap) {
            auto [b, oldsz] = st.back();
            st.pop_back();
            if (b == -1) continue;
            int a = p[b];
            p[b] = b;
            sz[a] = oldsz;
            comps++;
        }
    }
};

/*
Interpretation used:

Board interior is N x M white cells, surrounded by black border.
People can be placed on border cells:
- A: top side, length M
- B: right side, length N
- C: bottom side, length M
- D: left side, length N

If corresponding character is '1', a person starts there and moves inward:
- top -> downward through that column
- right -> leftward through that row
- bottom -> upward through that column
- left -> rightward through that row

All people move simultaneously, painting white cells black until the next cell would be non-white.
A final state is determined by an order-compatible outcome. This is equivalent to choosing, for each connected white component in the final board, from which sides it survives.
The count reduces to counting subsets of rays whose union of painted cells is realizable; equivalently,
for each cell graph, constraints come from opposite-directed rays intersecting.

This becomes counting connected components in a bipartite graph between active row-rays and column-rays:
- Left/right persons correspond to row constraints
- Top/bottom persons correspond to column constraints
Each interior cell is painted iff at least one of its row/column rays reaches it before blocked.
The final number of distinct states equals 2^(number of connected components in this active incidence graph), with isolated inactive vertices ignored.

The incidence graph edges are all active row-ray / column-ray pairs whose corresponding cell exists.
Since every active row-ray intersects every active column-ray, the graph is complete bipartite over groups induced by rows/cols.
But rows can have 0,1,2 active rays (from left/right), similarly columns from top/bottom.
Different rays on same row/column are distinct vertices.

Thus answer = 2^cc where cc is number of connected components in the bipartite graph
with:
row vertices = active chars in D and B
col vertices = active chars in A and C
edge between any active row vertex on row i and any active col vertex on col j.

Hence:
- if no active row or no active col: graph has all vertices isolated, so cc = total active vertices
- otherwise all active vertices belong to one connected component, so plus isolated none => cc = 1

This simplifies a lot.
*/

