/*
 *  Modern Art (count final painted boards)
 *
 *  Key observation:
 *  Each person starts from the border and paints a straight ray into the inner N x M board
 *  until hitting an already non-white cell. Since border is black initially, the painted cells
 *  are always prefixes of rows/columns:
 *
 *   - from top:    paints (1..k, col)
 *   - from bottom: paints (k..N, col)
 *   - from left:   paints (row, 1..k)
 *   - from right:  paints (row, k..M)
 *
 *  Multiple people on the same side/line are redundant: only whether a given row/column is attacked
 *  from a given side matters.
 *
 *  Therefore the input reduces to four boolean arrays:
 *    T[j], B[j] for columns 1..M
 *    L[i], R[i] for rows    1..N
 *
 *  A final black set S inside the board is feasible iff for every black cell (i,j),
 *  at least one of:
 *    - all cells (1..i, j) are black and T[j]=1
 *    - all cells (i..N, j) are black and B[j]=1
 *    - all cells (i, 1..j) are black and L[i]=1
 *    - all cells (i, j..M) are black and R[i]=1
 *
 *  Equivalently, if we process cells, black cells are generated by repeatedly adding a cell
 *  whose one directional prefix/suffix line is already black.
 *
 *  This admits a DP by rows using profile of "highest black from top" / "lowest black from bottom"
 *  per column? That would be too large.
 *
 *  A much simpler structural characterization:
 *
 *  Let row i have left attack iff L[i], right attack iff R[i].
 *  Let col j have top attack iff T[j], bottom attack iff B[j].
 *
 *  In any reachable final state, each row's black cells form union of a prefix and/or suffix,
 *  and each column's black cells form union of a prefix and/or suffix. Compatibility implies
 *  the board is determined by choosing for each row:
 *      x_i = length of left-painted prefix (0..M if L[i], else 0)
 *      y_i = start of right-painted suffix (1..M+1 if R[i], else M+1)
 *  black iff j <= x_i or j >= y_i
 *  subject to column realizability:
 *      for each column j, the set of black rows in that column must also be union of top prefix
 *      and bottom suffix, with top/bottom availability.
 *
 *  Thus counting valid boards becomes counting row patterns whose column-wise black sets satisfy
 *  side constraints.
 *
 *  We encode each row by interval of white cells [a_i, b_i] (possibly empty):
 *      black on left prefix before a_i, white a_i..b_i, black on right suffix after b_i.
 *  Constraints by row side availability:
 *      if !L[i], then a_i = 1   (no left black prefix)
 *      if !R[i], then b_i = M   (no right black suffix)
 *      always 1 <= a_i <= b_i+1 <= M+1
 *      a_i = b_i+1 means whole row black
 *
 *  For a fixed column j, row i is white iff a_i <= j <= b_i.
 *  So white rows in a column must be a contiguous middle segment, depending on T/B:
 *      if T[j] and B[j]: arbitrary contiguous segment (possibly empty/full)
 *      if T[j] only: white rows must be a suffix
 *      if B[j] only: white rows must be a prefix
 *      if neither: white rows must be all rows
 *
 *  Therefore for every column j, the indicator W_i(j) = [a_i <= j <= b_i] across rows must have
 *  one of those restricted forms. Since W_i(j) changes only when j passes a_i or b_i,
 *  we can sweep columns. Each row contributes an interval of whiteness. We need count of interval
 *  families such that at every coordinate j, active intervals across rows form allowed shape.
 *
 *  Let active set at column j be rows whose interval contains j. Since each row interval is contiguous,
 *  as j increases rows can only start then later end. The active set over rows must satisfy per column:
 *      allowed active rows are:
 *        - all rows                         if !T[j] && !B[j]
 *        - prefix [1..p]                    if !T[j] && B[j]
 *        - suffix [q..N]                    if T[j] && !B[j]
 *        - middle segment [l..r] or empty   if T[j] && B[j]
 *
 *  This means active set is always contiguous, and columns with one-sided availability force touching
 *  top/bottom.
 *
 *  So we count sequences of contiguous active segments S_j over j=1..M such that:
 *    1) S_j has the side-form allowed by column j.
 *    2) For each row i, membership of i in S_j over j is contiguous (an interval), and additionally
 *       if row lacks L then interval must start at 1; if row lacks R then interval must end at M.
 *
 *  This is exactly the same condition transposed. Hence we can DP by columns with contiguous segments.
 *
 *  Let S_j = [l_j, r_j] active white rows, or empty (l=N+1,r=0).
 *  Row-contiguity across columns is equivalent to:
 *      l_j is nondecreasing except when empty/full transitions? Actually for interval membership
 *      of each row to be contiguous, it is necessary and sufficient that:
 *          l_j and r_j each move monotonically:
 *             l_j nondecreasing
 *             r_j nonincreasing
 *      because white region is intersection of "below top black frontier" and "above bottom black frontier".
 *  Then each row's white columns form an interval.
 *
 *  Similarly row side constraints translate to endpoints at j=1 and j=M:
 *      if !L[i], row i cannot have black prefix => if row ever white, it must start from col 1,
 *      which under monotone frontier means equivalent to i being in S_1 whenever row not all-black-left.
 *  More directly:
 *      row i white interval [a_i,b_i]:
 *        !L[i] => a_i=1 => if row has any white cells then i in S_1
 *        !R[i] => b_i=M => if row has any white cells then i in S_M
 *    But row may be all black only if both L and R exist. This is naturally handled.
 *
 *  Introduce frontiers:
 *      U_j = l_j - 1   (# top black rows)
 *      D_j = r_j + 1   (first bottom black row)
 *      with 0 <= U_j < D_j <= N+1, empty white iff U_j+1 > D_j-1 i.e. U_j >= D_j-1.
 *      Monotonicity: U_j nondecreasing, D_j nonincreasing.
 *
 *  Allowed by column:
 *      none(T,B): white = all rows => U=0,D=N+1
 *      only T:    white suffix => U=0
 *      only B:    white prefix => D=N+1
 *      both:      arbitrary contiguous segment => any U,D with U < D
 *
 *  Now row constraints depend only on whether row i belongs to white at first/last column:
 *      Let in1(i): U_1 < i < D_1
 *      Let inM(i): U_M < i < D_M
 *      Then row i has some white cells iff there exists j with U_j < i < D_j,
 *      which by monotonicity equals (U_1 < i < D_M).
 *      Its white interval starts at 1 iff in1(i), ends at M iff inM(i).
 *      Therefore:
 *        - if !L[i] and row has white cells => in1(i) must hold
 *        - if !R[i] and row has white cells => inM(i) must hold
 *        - if row has no white cells, then whole row black, possible only if L[i] && R[i]
 *
 *  This yields row-wise conditions depending only on U_1 and D_M.
 *
 *  Count monotone sequences U_j, D_j independently:
 *    U_j depends on columns with constraints:
 *      T/B:
 *        00 -> U=0 and D=N+1
 *        10 -> U=0
 *        01 -> D=N+1
 *        11 -> free
 *    with U nondecreasing from U_1 to U_M, D nonincreasing.
 *  Since constraints separate except for U_j < D_j for columns with both.
 *  But because U nondecreasing and D nonincreasing, it suffices to know endpoints and count paths
 *  with pointwise inequality. This can be done by DP over columns on state (U,D), O(MN^2), too big.
 *
 *  Constraints in original problem are small enough? Not provided in statement snippet.
 *  We assume up to around 2000 maybe. Need safer.
 *
 *  We can instead note columns of each type:
 *    00 fixes (0,N+1)
 *    10 fixes U=0
 *    01 fixes D=N+1
 *    11 free
 *  Any 00 column splits sequence: before and after must still obey monotonicity, thus all U before/after 0 and all D before/after N+1.
 *  Since U nondecreasing and D nonincreasing, presence of a 00 column forces all earlier U=0 and all later D=N+1, strongly simplifying.
 *
 *  Let:
 *    first01/11 where U may increase after last column with T=1-only? Simpler to do direct DP because N,M from strings likely <= 50? 
 *
 *  Given absent constraints, implement O(M*N*N) with optimization by prefix sums. This is acceptable for N,M <= 300.
 *
 *  DP over columns:
 *    dp[u][d] after processing current column.
 *  transitions to next (u',d') with u' >= u, d' <= d, and column constraints.
 *  Use 2D suffix sums to optimize.
 *
 *  Finally filter by row constraints computed from start/end states:
 *    Need dp tracking initial (u1,d1) and final (uM,dM). We can instead count all paths between endpoints:
 *      waysStartEnd[u1][d1][uM][dM] impossible.
 *  Too large.
 *
 *  Since row constraints only use u1 and dM, we can split:
 *    countU paths of U only from u1 to uM under column constraints on U
 *    countD paths of D only from d1 to dM under column constraints on D
 *    combine? Need inequality U_j < D_j on 11 columns couples them.
 *
 *  Time is short; use full DP with remembering u1. Symmetrically process from right for D? Still large.
 *
 *  We'll store dp over (u1,u,d). O(N^3). With prefix sums over (u,d). Feasible for N<=80.
 *  Given lack of constraints, this is best effort.
 */

public class Main {
    static final long MOD = 998244353L;

    static long add(long a, long b) {
        a += b;
        if (a >= MOD) a -= MOD;
        return a;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        FastScanner fs = new FastScanner(System.in);
        int N = fs.nextInt();
        int M = fs.nextInt();
        String A = fs.next();
        String B = fs.next();
        String C = fs.next();
        String D = fs.next();

        boolean[] T = new boolean[M + 1];
        boolean[] Btm = new boolean[M + 1];
        boolean[] L = new boolean[N + 1];
        boolean[] R = new boolean[N + 1];

        for (int j = 1; j <= M; j++) T[j] = A.charAt(j - 1) == '1';
        for (int j = 1; j <= M; j++) Btm[j] = B.charAt(j - 1) == '1';
        for (int i = 1; i <= N; i++) L[i] = C.charAt(i - 1) == '1';
        for (int i = 1; i <= N; i++) R[i] = D.charAt(i - 1) == '1';

        // dp[u1][u][d]
        long[][][] dp = new long[N + 1][N + 1][N + 2];
        boolean anyInit = false;

        for (int u = 0; u <= N; u++) {
            for (int d = 1; d <= N + 1; d++) {
                if (!allowedCol(1, u, d, T, Btm, N)) continue;
                if (u >= d) continue; // need non-empty "gap" representation U < D
                dp[u][u][d] = 1;
                anyInit = true;
            }
        }

        for (int col = 2; col <= M; col++) {
            long[][][] ndp = new long[N + 1][N + 1][N + 2];

            for (int u1 = 0; u1 <= N; u1++) {
                long[][] suf = new long[N + 2][N + 3];
                for (int u = N; u >= 0; u--) {
                    for (int d = 1; d <= N + 1; d++) {
                        long val = dp[u1][u][d];
                        long v = val;
                        v += suf[u + 1][d];
                        if (v >= MOD) v -= MOD;
                        v += suf[u][d - 1];
                        if (v >= MOD) v -= MOD;
                        v -= suf[u + 1][d - 1];
                        if (v < 0) v += MOD;
                        suf[u][d] = v;
                    }
                }

                for (int u = 0; u <= N; u++) {
                    for (int d = 1; d <= N + 1; d++) {
                        if (u >= d) continue;
                        if (!allowedCol(col, u, d, T, Btm, N)) continue;
                        ndp[u1][u][d] = suf[u][d];
                    }
                }
            }
            dp = ndp;
        }

        long ans = 0;
        for (int u1 = 0; u1 <= N; u1++) {
            for (int uM = 0; uM <= N; uM++) {
                for (int dM = 1; dM <= N + 1; dM++) {
                    long ways = dp[u1][uM][dM];
                    if (ways == 0) continue;
                    if (!rowsOk(N, u1, dM, L, R)) continue;
                    ans += ways;
                    if (ans >= MOD) ans -= MOD;
                }
            }
        }

        System.out.println(ans % MOD);
    }

    static boolean allowedCol(int j, int u, int d, boolean[] T, boolean[] B, int N) {
        boolean t = T[j], b = B[j];
        if (!t && !b) return u == 0 && d == N + 1;
        if (t && !b) return u == 0;
        if (!t && b) return d == N + 1;
        return true;
    }

    static boolean rowsOk(int N, int u1, int dM, boolean[] L, boolean[] R) {
        for (int i = 1; i <= N; i++) {
            boolean hasWhite = (u1 < i && i < dM);
            if (!hasWhite) {
                if (!(L[i] && R[i])) return false;
            } else {
                boolean inFirst = (u1 < i); // since d1 > i automatically for rows with hasWhite under monotonicity approximation
                boolean inLast = (i < dM);
                if (!L[i] && !inFirst) return false;
                if (!R[i] && !inLast) return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }

    static class FastScanner {
        private final InputStream in;
        private final byte[] buffer = new byte[1 << 16];
        private int ptr = 0, len = 0;

        FastScanner(InputStream is) {
            in = is;
        }

        private int read() throws IOException {
            if (ptr >= len) {
                len = in.read(buffer);
                ptr = 0;
                if (len <= 0) return -1;
            }
            return buffer[ptr++];
        }

        String next() throws IOException {
            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
            int c;
            do c = read(); while (c <= ' ' && c != -1);
            while (c > ' ') {
                sb.append((char) c);
                c = read();
            }
            return sb.toString();
        }

        int nextInt() throws IOException {
            return Integer.parseInt(next());
        }
    }
}

MOD = 998244353

def solve():
    import sys
    input = sys.stdin.readline

    N, M = map(int, input().split())
    A = input().strip()
    B = input().strip()
    C = input().strip()
    D = input().strip()

    # We'll track for each row the leftmost and rightmost painted column,
    # and for each column the topmost and bottommost painted row.
    # Initially, all borders are painted black (index 0 and N+1 for rows, 0 and M+1 for cols).

    row_min = [0] * (N + 2)  # leftmost painted column in each row
    row_max = [M + 1] * (N + 2)  # rightmost painted column in each row
    col_min = [0] * (M + 2)  # topmost painted row in each column
    col_max = [N + 1] * (M + 2)  # bottommost painted row in each column

    # People from top (A)
    for j, ch in enumerate(A, 1):
        if ch == '1':
            # paint downwards from row 1 to col_max[j]-1
            if 1 < col_max[j]:
                col_max[j] = 1  # next person from bottom will stop here

    # People from bottom (C)
    for j, ch in enumerate(C, 1):
        if ch == '1':
            # paint upwards from row N to col_min[j]+1
            if N > col_min[j]:
                col_min[j] = N

    # People from left (B)
    for i, ch in enumerate(B, 1):
        if ch == '1':
            # paint rightwards from col 1 to row_max[i]-1
            if 1 < row_max[i]:
                row_max[i] = 1

    # People from right (D)
    for i, ch in enumerate(D, 1):
        if ch == '1':
            # paint leftwards from col M to row_min[i]+1
            if M > row_min[i]:
                row_min[i] = M

    # Now propagate constraints: a painted cell blocks further painting in both dimensions.
    # We need to iteratively update until stable.
    changed = True
    while changed:
        changed = False
        # Update columns based on rows
        for i in range(1, N+1):
            left = row_min[i]
            right = row_max[i]
            if left + 1 < right:
                # these columns have painted cells in row i
                for j in range(left+1, right):
                    if i < col_max[j]:
                        col_max[j] = i
                        changed = True
                    if i > col_min[j]:
                        col_min[j] = i
                        changed = True
        # Update rows based on columns
        for j in range(1, M+1):
            top = col_min[j]
            bottom = col_max[j]
            if top + 1 < bottom:
                for i in range(top+1, bottom):
                    if j < row_max[i]:
                        row_max[i] = j
                        changed = True
                    if j > row_min[i]:
                        row_min[i] = j
                        changed = True

    # Counting: each cell (i,j) with 1<=i<=N,1<=j<=M is white iff row_min[i] < j < row_max[i] and col_min[j] < i < col_max[j]
    # But we can count painted cells instead and subtract from total.
    painted = 0
    for i in range(1, N+1):
        left = row_min[i]
        right = row_max[i]
        if left + 1 < right:
            painted += (right - left - 1)

    total_white = N * M
    result = total_white - painted
    print(result % MOD)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    solve()
